{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: exact head f7b65e66c78ac2dc29b09f494782adbe68ed2a60 on planner/sequenced-amendment-20261110, both artifacts, for request ffbea8ed under promise 432cdd57 and review request 7d98017f. The design holds. I accept the kernel exception of ruling 08c5ff25, so the level does not wait on that point. Six statements in the note contradict main or the note itself, and an implementer would build two of them into the contract wrongly.\n\nThe kernel review, which section 7 of `docs/kernel.md` asks for:\n\n- The argument holds. A sequenced input's result depends on what it read at a cut. Runtime and Replay must derive the same page, the same prepare effects and the same effective fact, so the read and the entry kinds belong in `contract` and `derive`. A service can deliver a member's state only as unordered messages, and an application cannot read another scope.\n- The exception is narrow. Ruling 08c5ff25 rests on the owner's decision in order fc7576f8, covers only the first version of 5.11, closes no K item, and counts `order` in K4 and `platform:order@1` in K1. The kernel.md text says the same in sections 7 and 7.1. I found no other path in section 7.1, and the precedent of ruling 1bc4f7fd has the same form.\n- The first version is a sound cut. It leaves out handlers, `staleness`, `leave`, `close`, child creation and clone replay, and the table of 5.11 says what the level does without each. A hole and expiry stay, so a stopped member cannot hold a position open forever.\n\nRequired changes:\n\n1. `expired` already means something else. The resolve request answers `expired` (5.3, lines 428-448; 5.4 step 8; 5.8 line 802). `RefusalReason` already has `expired`: \"the commit clock is at or past `notAfter`\" (`packages/contract/src/result.ts:21`). Step 8 uses the word in both senses within a few lines. Give the allocation's state and the refusal a name of their own, for example `allocation-expired`, and add it to the reason list in 13.2.\n2. One condition has two names. An allocation in a `forming` group is refused `order-forming` (lines 380, 429, and the list in 13.2). The delivery table at line 572 lists the same condition as `forming`. Use `order-forming` in both places.\n3. The `at` page has two bounds. Line 623 says the page is \"bounded as `items` is\". The items page is `RETAINED_ITEMS_PAGE = 100` (`packages/contract/src/read.ts:35`). Lines 701 and 838 and O8 give 64, the contract's list bound. Choose one bound and state it once.\n4. The package check names a field that does not exist. The check compares the root's `name`, `size` and `lifetime` with the `groups` entry (lines 392-395). `PackageGroup` (`packages/package-graph/src/types.ts:6`) has `name`, `members` and `lifetime`, and no `size`. Say that `size` equals the length of `members`. `PACKAGE_COMPOSITION_LIMITS.members` is 128 (`types.ts:36`), and 5.11 and the order scope allow 32. Say that H5 refuses a group with more than 32 members.\n5. A wrong section reference. Line 16 cites \"section 15.2, point 5 of its decision\" of the archive note. The five-point decision is in 15.1 (`notes/2026-10-09-shared-language-volatile-acts.md`, from line 1397). Section 8 of the note already says 15.1.\n6. `docs/kernel.md` describes 13.2 in two ways that 13.2 does not support. It says 13.2 gives \"the words that promises 2, 4 and 5 take\". 13.2 quotes new words for promises 4 and 5 and only describes promise 2: nine inputs become fifteen. Either quote promise 2's new sentence in 13.2 or say \"the change to promise 2\". kernel.md also says the change happens \"when replay handles it\", and 13.2 says that H6 makes it. Use one of the two.\n\nSmaller corrections, which the next head can take with the six above:\n\n- Section 7 says that `room.alerts` relates to `site.alerts` with `copies` 32 and that `site.presence` fans out to the rooms. At main, `examples/grow/site.ts` has a `room-summary` relate with `copies: 3`, and presence is an item each member owns. 13.1 says that the amendment removed the contradictions with main. Either correct the table or mark it as the design's example, not main.\n- 5.5 says that `at` answers `busy`. `ReadRefusal` has no `busy`. It is an `UnavailableReason` (`result.ts:47`). Say which answer type carries it.\n- 5.8 says that an `incomplete` report names the prepare entry in `at`. `Report.at` is documented as \"the entry a mismatch names\" (`packages/replay/src/report.ts:27`). Say that `at` gains this second use, or name the entry elsewhere.\n- The text before the heads table says that the package-graph part of H5 needs only H1. The table gives H5 \"Needs: H3\". Split the row, or say which part waits for H3.\n- For H1: the `read` operand reads another member's slots. The rule `redactable-read` (`packages/derive/src/validate/operands.ts:204`) refuses a guard that reads a detached text. The note does not say that a `read` of another member's detached text is refused in the same way. State it in 5.2, or give it to H1's refusal pairs.\n\nWhat holds:\n\n- Scope. Main 73e30699 is an ancestor, the head is pushed, and the planner's worktree is clean at the head. Against main, the head changes exactly the two paths that its two artifacts cover, both Markdown. `check-notes` reports 0 warnings. The commit-message check passes: 1 checked, 0 flagged. `git diff --check` is clean. On my own extracted copy, `check-doc-links.mjs` checks 1,227 local links and finds none broken.\n- The citations. A separate read at main checked 27 cited files, about 45 symbols and quotes, about 30 section references and seven commits. Every cited file and symbol exists. The checks include `DurabilityLevel` naming `sequenced`, `validateFormat1` refusing every level but recorded version 1, the six derive functions that set `self`, `admitChildCreation` refusing a lane's platform child, `judgeFloor` keeping 100 entries, the `stagedFindings` comparison that never holds, `BehindRead`, and the kernel promises 2, 4, 5 and 11 as quoted. The `SequenceRef` and `FactRef` shapes match section 8 of the identifiers note. The new answers `uncommitted` and `cut-gone` collide with nothing in `contract`.\n- The gaps. Gap 1 (the root creates the order scope through one new lineage edge, and the package check binds its constants), gap 2 (cut copies of the newest 8 cuts, `cut-gone` beyond), gap 3 (13.2), gap 4 (13.3) and gap 5 (13.4) each have one answer in one place. Decisions 22 to 29 are numbered without a gap.\n\nArchitecture: sound, apart from the six corrections. The level is kernel for the reason 13.2 gives, the order rules stay platform code, and a new creation family carries the pin, so no existing room changes. The six heads follow the dependency order and keep one gate each.\n\nSecurity: no blocking finding. Only a `joined` member of the same order scope reads at a cut, with its own authorization. Pages are bounded, and expiry ends every open allocation. A group without `leave` keeps a member that never returns. Its readers then see `member-uncommitted` for the group's life, and 5.11 says so. The detached-text point above belongs in H1.\n\nSimplification: no further change. The first version leaves out six parts, and each one keeps a stated fallback.\n\nThis verdict authorizes no landing. The kernel exception stands under ruling 08c5ff25, and a new head with the six required changes can come back for review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f698d5e293c210adfe27d56404beb24b24a0a9f7","binding":"assigned","head":"f7b65e66c78ac2dc29b09f494782adbe68ed2a60","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6b51a14844e6bcaf64955c1452e40cc62fde84e0\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:432cdd57ecc953b1eaf48ebd276cf15a45e5926e\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6b51a14844e6bcaf64955c1452e40cc62fde84e0","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}